3 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8208cfa-7eb3-58e5-ba4e-3c6c7623b96c" :question/math-academy-id "q-317355" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-4{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-3?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=4{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/c9/c925aea9381d9ecb008b7ab609ef72817a7c8bc51a5e3ab5abf4fb33f9055730.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-4{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,4)$ to $(0,-4)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/fa/fa429123677e529f376c871642e0c4c0986cb5ea5319f7bf59b225ea0d36f792.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $3$ units down to give $y=-4{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-3.$ The point $(0,-4)$ moves to $(0,-7)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](images/35/3511221e88625a10cc41b1a1470c13c38ef9398afb88fa09ae2f033e88ad9696.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115369 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "623f4440-ba1a-58ce-9ff2-ccdf591a0d30" :knowledge-point/title "Vertical Reflections of Exponential Decay Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aea8813-2274-5a0e-900a-f3cb9e331517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d8/d8c6d83734b0e8e8b227c66a20db2092ebe1530bc20831d500515e0785561cd9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390675} {:answer/id #uuid "c6aab864-1ea7-581f-bbab-1705433414fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b1/b186111aafb5379a3f35183e56d891bb8fa987047aacef6fe0e40e3e497c2c46.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390676} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc74888-38ca-50e1-9f3f-1caa58604247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident